; G14 pointer builtins — read and write path. HIR channel parity.
; Semantics under test:
;   - `&x` yields a pointer to a snapshot of the variable's value
;   - `store(px, v)` encodes v into the pointed-to memory
;   - a later `load(px)` observes what the store wrote
;
; Scope: scalar I64 only, and `x` is never printed after the store.
; Both restrictions are the same boundary: the AST arm snapshots while the LLVM
; arm may take a true reference, so the two disagree only if the fixture reads
; the *variable* after writing through the pointer. Keeping that read out means
; the fixture measures the pointer nodes rather than the snapshot/reference
; split, which belongs to the address-of grid. Narrow element types are also out
; of scope: the snapshot path infers the element type from the value while the
; lowerer infers it from the declaration, so the two can only differ where the
; declared width is narrower than the value's.

main|func() -> ():
    var x: I64 = 7
    var px = unsafe &x
    print(unsafe load(px))
    unsafe store(px, 42)
    print(unsafe load(px))
    return
